    "available_at": "2026-11-17T15:01:46.889533+00:00",
    "body": "Seven in 10 manufacturing specialists still use spreadsheets or lists to track whether their products meet compliance requirements, according to a recent Assent study . Only 31% said they use software built specifically for product compliance.\nWhat’s Related\nThe biggest reporting problem was the information itself. Fifty-seven percent cited incomplete or unreliable data as a major challenge in meeting product compliance and sustainability reporting requirements. Limited staffing came close behind at 53%, while 48% cited regulatory complexity.\nAssent and Germany’s Pforzheim University surveyed 131 manufacturing specialists and managers from April through June 2026, including respondents in the automotive and electronics industries, and supplemented the survey with seven expert interviews.\nWhere supplier data falls short\nThe study looked at what happens when manufacturers ask suppliers for data on carbon emissions . Incomplete information and late responses tied as the most common problems, each cited by 55% of respondents. Forty-five percent said suppliers received unclear instructions about what data to provide, and 40% pointed to a lack of automation for submitting responses.\nThe report described how manufacturers can make those requests easier to answer. A request can spell out which parts it covers and what emissions the supplier should count. A common submission process can also spare suppliers from entering the same information in different formats.\nRELATED STORIES: Manufacturers Say Supervisors Waste 4 Hours Per Shift on Data | 10 Routine Supply Chain Tasks You Should Use AI For\nHow much time manual work takes\nSixty-nine percent of respondents said they spent up to two hours a day on repetitive manual work, including 34% who reported spending one to two hours. Another 15% reported spending three to four hours a day on those tasks. About one-third described their current processes as efficient.\nSpreadsheets were the most common tool, even though 58% reported using enterprise resource planning systems, the software companies use to manage functions such as purchasing and inventory. Respondents could select more than one tool, so a company could use a central system and still rely on spreadsheets for some compliance work.\nAI draws interest, but no one is using it\nAI and automation ranked as the biggest opportunity in the survey, but only 10% said they had built AI into their processes. Among respondents whose systems already shared data, 72% saw AI as an opportunity, compared with 48% of those whose systems were separate.\nTechnology and software led the list of investment priorities at 43%, followed by connecting data and IT systems at 35%. Assent said those connections matter because AI tools draw on records about parts, finished products, and suppliers. If those records are incomplete or kept in separate systems, the tools have less reliable information to work with.",
